text_output=Good morning, everyone. Good morning. Let's get started. So welcome to our Q3 26 QGS agenda topics today. We'll talk about three. One, I want to share our engineering strategy to realize the business growth that Alex spoke about in his last QGS. We'll then talk about engineering priorities and just general execution, and then Lisa will join me and go through all of the work that's done with the software team, who I think is probably one of the largest pivots to do to go enable the growth ahead of us that Alex spoke to. And then at the end we'll have plenty of time for Q&A, both in room and online. Okay, so you recall Alex spoke about business growth, about client, agentic, edge, and physical AI. So the goal of this slide is to describe our strategy on a page, how we as an engineering organization enable that growth. Again, we are the scale function that will drive growth for all of these business units. So the way we look at a simplified stack is just meant to describe what are the competitive advantages and the strategy by which we will enable our businesses to grow. It'll guide our decision making and our principles, and it's a simplified view. But one thing that's very important to understand is it's not about optimizing one layer above all else. It's looking at this whole stack end to end to bring a platform leadership at scale with quality and predictable execution. Like as an example, silicon by itself without the software value to unlock it, it's not a good platform play. Right. So starting at the bottom, process and packaging, our strategy will and has been to maximize the IDM advantage. So working closely with TD on how we can optimize for performance, for cost, and for power. I would say that's a strategy that's well in motion. If you look at all the PRQs we did as an example back in 24, that was predominantly external silicon. You contrast that to what we're going to do in Hammerlick, and I'll show on the roadmap later on, where 100% internal silicon, and that's a function of working with Intel Foundry on progressing 18A, 18AP, Intel 3 on yield, performance, and power. That does not mean we won't use external options where we got to augment our business. That could be in situations like chipsets where we use Samsung technology or Wi-Fi, or if we need to do a dual drive because we have limitations. But primary strategy is IDM advantage, leverage that, good for good for us, good for Intel. Sitting above that then a silicon roadmap, job there is simple, deliver leadership, silicon, IP, SoC at scale, right? And the scale is very important. We see the benefit of that today, even in our traditional
